this morning on the dish atlanta  restaurateur justin anthony he wasn't a  trained chef but he always thought the  food of his native south africa might be  a big hit in this country  and he was right expanding to a handful  of eateries in atlanta  then the pandemic hit and he turned much  of his attention to feeding his  community  prepping and delivering meals to  frontline healthcare workers  at hospitals near his biltong bar one of  those eateries  just before that turn of events i had  the chance to sit down with anthony as  we all used to do  and enjoy a culinary tour of his  homeland  so welcome to beef jerky and booze i  love it here at biltong bar  justin anthony brings the street food of  his native south africa  to atlanta bolton is different than beef  jerky and then it's air dried  kind of like a dry aged steak so right  now we've got our traditional right here  okay  this one right here is peri-peri so the  it's going to be spicy coming for that  the cured meat is a south african staple  brought by the dutch four centuries ago  what's interesting is that  literally on every street corner every  butcher you go into  there's just bolton everywhere but it's  very much  only exist in south africa and  she says south africa's unique cuisines  are a direct result of its makeup south  africa has 11 11 official languages it's  the same thing with the food so you've  got french portuguese  dutch german english indian malaysian  and mediterranean influences so like  there's so much going on  if somebody asked you now what is south  african cuisine  in a sentence what would you say south  african cuisine  is it's a fusion of i hate using the  word fusion basically it's like by  taking your taste buds on safari  anthony would eventually bring that  safari to america  but it was sports that brought him here  almost 25 years ago  i moved to atlanta back in 96  just before the olympics and i moved  here to actually play professional  soccer  so that's how i ended up in atlanta and  unfortunately  or maybe fortunately for all of us  soccer didn't work  yeah i had a knee injury and i was  struggling to play at the level that i  wanted to play at  so i made a conscious decision that i  was going to retire uncertain about  what career might be next anthony had an  epiphany  in the mid to late 90s there were a lot  of south africans moving to atlanta and  i was like you know what i've been  always been passionate about food and  wine my parents are chefs and i kind of  grew up around it and so  i was like i'm gonna open up a south  african pub restaurant  and everyone thought i was crazy because  it's not that easy to do  this was 97 1997 there was like  no such thing as a south african  restaurant so literally i went from  being a professional soccer player to  being  a waiter at a restaurant just kind of  learning the business all of these  dishes have been a staple since day one  anthony who's an only child convinced  his parents diane and derek  to leave johannesburg and join him here  in the states  i mean now that i think about it it was  terrifying but  i mean you know we did what we had to do  right  to make ourselves happy and to be with  justin  that's what parents do isn't it together  they crafted a menu that encompasses the  many flavors and spices of home  in 1998 they open what they believe was  the first  south african restaurant in the u.s 10  degrees south  which is south africa's location from  the tropic of capricorn  let's dig in we share dishes like  chicken curry  perry perry chicken and calamari what  was it really like  in the early days of first of all trying  to come up with  this but then also working with your son  it's a different  thing to work with family than working  with other people  i think it worked well i mean we were  just so busy  trying to make things work and we were  doing everything i mean every single one  of us knew how to bartend  serve be in the kitchen all of us  now along with 10 degrees south anthony  operates  four restaurant concepts in atlanta  including biltong bar  yebo beach house and cape dutch every  time i eat curry or perry perry i start  sweating  but he calls 10 degrees south the  mothership with foods at his other spots  stemming from it you notice i'm not  being shy i'm just reaching for it yeah  i think it is go through  in south africa we have a saying get  stuck in  that's it just take the whole thing  and the right way to eat the floor yeah  just take it out the shell  right chew the shell and suck it so you  get the taste and so you can really get  and you get the real taste well i  wouldn't say it's the right way  well i mean it's his way  did you ever imagine when your son was  playing soccer  that this would become the future no  i thought that he was going to do sports  psychology  and that was it and instead  a family business  [Applause]  the anthony family has hosted a  veritable who's who  from morgan freeman and robert de niro  to aaron rodgers  and lebron james which dessert am i  about to try  it's called a lot this is the  this is the lebron james dessert is what  somebody who's an nba fan would say  it's a a riff if you will of a  traditional south african dessert called  malba pudding  oh my gosh even without the ice cream  would be  delicious yes ron james famously ate  i think seven or eight of them before  dinner yeah why not really play for the  heat they were in town  anthony says it's his sports background  that team philosophy and work ethic he  had on the field  which played a big part of building  success  in the restaurant industry there's not  such thing as an easy business you want  to be a successful athlete  you better work hard you want to be a  successful business owner you better  work hard yeah the harder you work the  luckier you get nothing happens  easy you have to work for it to know  that you came here to play soccer  and you've created a family business of  restaurants  for you what's that like you know  sometimes i have to pinch myself  it's kind of the american dream and the  name of the restaurant group true story  brands and he says that's because it is  a true story that they were able to  build this here in america the  american dream i really miss our dishes  you hate to sit there  i know it's so close like that  pre-pandemic yeah

